Original commit body from 7ffd9a2236947c09b58bd34143dd43cbad6ad784:

run_survey's max_files is documented as a per-language-analyzer cap. It reached
only the 10 of 119 analyzers registered supports_max_files=True; 108 take
repo_root alone, so the cap could not travel as an argument. At --max-files 1
this repository still analyzed 252 files (the item's count).

The cap is now a per-analyzer FileBudget, a context variable that _run_named
opens in each analyzer's worker thread and discovery.find_files consults on
both of its paths (index and rglob). Admission is by file: a file admitted
once is admitted again, so a two-pass analyzer sees the same N files in both
passes. The budget binds only its own thread, so linkers and parallel
analyzers are not charged.

Four finders read the file index directly and are now bounded through
within_file_budget, which runs after a candidate is known to be the
analyzer's:
- bash's shebang scan of extensionless files (33 files at cap 1);
- gitignore's unconditional root .gitignore (2 at cap 1);
- the generic yaml finder;
- yaml_ansible, charged where its analyzer consumes the files and not inside
  find_ansible_files, because the generic yaml analyzer calls that finder
  for the set it skips.

Measured on this repository at --max-files 1 after the change: the 49
analyzer runs read 24 files in total, and none read more than 1. Still not
bounded, and now said in --help and the run_survey docstring: linkers (15
passes read more than 1 file) and the opt-in SCIP backends, which hand the
whole repository to an external indexer. Every file an analyzer finds through
the finder counts against the cap, a manifest or header lookup included.

Also: the generic yaml analyzer's run reported files_analyzed 0 beside N file
anchors. It now reports N.

